  "UIExploreWhatTitle": "What Latchwire does",
  "UIExploreWhat": "Latchwire connects this computer to a WireGuard server, a private VPN that you or someone you trust runs. It is a desktop app for the tunnel: it creates the key, keeps it on a YubiKey and starts and stops the connection.",
  "UIExploreWhyTitle": "Why the key cannot be copied",
  "UIExploreWhy": "The key is created inside the YubiKey and the card does the key math itself, so the private key never reaches this computer's disk or a backup. A stolen or imaged laptop without the card cannot complete a handshake. This stops copying, not using: while the card is plugged in and unlocked, malware running as you could still use the key.",
  "UIExploreNeedTitle": "What you need",
  "UIExploreNeed": "A YubiKey with firmware 5.7 or newer, which supports X25519 in its PIV application, and a WireGuard server you can reach. A server administrator has to add your public key to the server; Latchwire does not connect to the server for you.",
  "UIExploreNext": "Next",
  "UIExploreBack": "Back",
  "UIExploreReady": "Ready, let's start",
  "UIExploreSite": "Read more at latchwire.com",
  "UIRoadmapWaitTitle": "Continue when your YubiKey arrives",
  "UIRoadmapWait": "Latchwire needs a YubiKey with firmware 5.7 or newer; older ones cannot do X25519 in PIV. Check the model's details before you get one. Yubico's product page lists the current models. Come back to the steps below once you have one.",
  "UIRoadmapWaitLink": "Open Yubico's product page",
  "UIRoadmapWaitHave": "I have a YubiKey now",
  "UIRoadmapUnsure": "Not sure your YubiKey will work? It needs firmware 5.7 or newer, which supports X25519 in PIV. Plug it in: Latchwire shows its firmware on the next screen, and an older key is not offered for key creation.",
  "UIExplainOnboardLong": "What happens here: Latchwire creates a new WireGuard key inside the YubiKey, in an empty slot. The card does the key math itself and never gives the private key out, so nobody can copy it off the card. The card's PIN unlocks the key; Latchwire asks for it and does not store it. While the card is plugged in and unlocked, programs on this computer can use the key, but none can take a copy of it.",
  "UIExplainOnboardShort": "The key is made inside the card and cannot be copied off it; the PIN unlocks it.",
  "UIExplainPubkeyLong": "What happens here: a WireGuard key has two halves. The public key below is not secret: the server needs it to recognize this computer, and you can send it by e-mail or chat. The private key stays inside the card. The tunnel IP is this computer's address inside the tunnel. It must be a free address in the server's tunnel network, for example 10.8.0.2 when the server is 10.8.0.1.",
  "UIExplainPubkeyShort": "Give the server only this public key; it is not secret. The tunnel IP must be free on the server.",
  "UIExplainGuideLong": "What happens here: your server lets in only the peers it knows. Add this computer as a peer by following the steps on your server. The values to enter have a Copy button; none of them is secret. Latchwire does not connect to your server to do this for you.",
  "UIExplainGuideShort": "Add this computer as a peer on your server with the values below.",
  "UIGlossaryPeerTitle": "Peer",
  "UIGlossaryPeer": "A peer is the other end of a WireGuard tunnel. On your server, this computer is one peer, known by its public key and its tunnel IP.",
  "UIGlossaryEndpointTitle": "Endpoint",
  "UIGlossaryEndpoint": "The endpoint is where the server is reached from the internet: its public address or host name and its UDP port, for example vpn.example.com:51820.",
  "UIGlossaryAllowedIPsTitle": "AllowedIPs",
  "UIGlossaryAllowedIPs": "On the server, a peer's AllowedIPs is the tunnel address that peer may use: here your tunnel IP with /32, a single address. On this computer, AllowedIPs lists the networks that go through the tunnel.",
  "UIGlossaryPINTitle": "PIN and PUK",
  "UIGlossaryPIN": "The PIN unlocks the key on the card. After too many wrong tries (3 by default) the card blocks the PIN, and only the PUK can unblock it. Both belong to the card; Latchwire never stores them.",
  "UIGlossarySlotTitle": "Slot",
  "UIGlossarySlot": "A slot is a numbered place on the card that holds one key. Latchwire uses an empty retired slot (82 to 95) and leaves slots 9a and 9d alone.",
  "UIImportDeleteHint": "This file may contain secrets, such as a preshared key. Delete it once the tunnel is added.",
  "UIGuideTitle": "Add this computer to your server",
  "UIGuideIntro": "These steps add this computer as a peer to a WireGuard server that is already set up.",
  "UIGuideVersionNote": "Written for {product} {version}. Menu and field names are given in English, as the server shows them; other versions may differ slightly.",
  "UIGuideOpen": "Open the server guide",
  "UIGuideShow": "Show the guide",
  "UIGuideSomeoneElse": "Someone else does this step",
  "UIGuideDone": "Done, the server has my key",
  "UIGuideAllowedLabel": "Allowed IPs (this computer's tunnel IP)",
  "UIGuideCommandLabel": "Command",
  "UIGuideNoIP": "Enter this computer's tunnel IP on the public key screen first.",
  "UIGuideBackupNote": "You have a backup card: add it as a second peer the same way, with its own key and tunnel IP.",
  "UIGuideOPNsense1": "In the web interface, open VPN → WireGuard → Peers and click + to add a peer.",
  "UIGuideOPNsense2": "Fill in Name with a name you recognize, Public key and Allowed IPs with the values below, and in Instances pick the instance this computer connects to. Leave Pre-shared key, Endpoint address and Endpoint port empty.",
  "UIGuideOPNsense3": "Click Save, then Apply.",
  "UIGuideOPNsense4": "For the next step you need the server's details: on VPN → WireGuard → Instances, edit the instance and note its Public key and Listen port. The endpoint is the firewall's public address or host name with that port.",
  "UIGuidePfSense1": "In the web interface, open VPN → WireGuard → Peers and click Add Peer.",
  "UIGuidePfSense2": "Pick the Tunnel this computer connects to, fill in Description with a name you recognize, and Public Key and Allowed IPs with the values below (mask 32). Leave Dynamic Endpoint on and Pre-shared Key empty.",
  "UIGuidePfSense3": "Click Save Peer, then Apply Changes.",
  "UIGuidePfSense4": "For the next step you need the server's details: on VPN → WireGuard → Tunnels, edit the tunnel and note its Public Key and Listen Port. The endpoint is the firewall's public address or host name with that port.",
  "UIGuideMikroTik1": "Open a terminal on the router (New Terminal in WinBox, Terminal in WebFig, or SSH) and run the command below. Use your WireGuard interface's name if it is not wireguard1.",
  "UIGuideMikroTik2": "Or without the terminal: in WebFig open WireGuard → Peers → Add New (in WinBox: WireGuard → Peers → +), pick the Interface, fill in Public Key and Allowed Address with the values below and click OK.",
  "UIGuideMikroTik3": "For the next step you need the server's details: run /interface/wireguard/print and note the public-key and listen-port of that interface. The endpoint is the router's public address or host name with that port.",
  "UIGuideLinux1": "On the server, add the block below to the end of /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf. Use your interface's name instead of wg0 if it differs.",
  "UIGuideLinux2": "Load the change without dropping the other peers:",
  "UIGuideLinux3": "For the next step you need the server's details: run sudo wg show wg0 and note the public key and listening port. The endpoint is the server's public address or host name with that port.",
  "UIGuideOther1": "Find the screen where your server adds a WireGuard peer. It needs three things: a name you recognize, the public key below and the allowed IPs below. Leave the peer's endpoint empty: this computer connects to the server, not the other way round.",
  "UIGuideOther2": "Save the peer and apply the change the way your server asks for.",
  "UIGuideOther3": "For the next step you need the server's details: its own public key and listen port. The endpoint is the server's public address or host name with that port.",
  "UIShareSubject": "Please add me to your WireGuard server",
  "UIShareIntro": "Hello,\n\nI connect to our VPN with Latchwire. The private key stays on a YubiKey and cannot be copied out of it, so only the public key below goes to you. Please add this computer to your WireGuard server as a peer.",
  "UIShareKeyMain": "Key 1, my main YubiKey",
  "UIShareKeyBackup": "Key 2, my backup YubiKey (add it as a second peer with its own tunnel IP)",
  "UIShareKeyLine": "Public key: {key}",
  "UIShareIPLine": "Tunnel IP: {ip}",
  "UIShareIPFree": "your choice: an unused address in the tunnel network, with /32",
  "UIShareHow": "How to add a peer (the [Peer] block above has the values):",
  "UIShareHowOPNsense": "OPNsense: VPN > WireGuard > Peers > +; fill Public key and Allowed IPs, pick the instance in Instances, leave Endpoint empty, Save and Apply.",
  "UIShareHowPfSense": "pfSense: VPN > WireGuard > Peers > Add Peer; pick the tunnel, fill Public Key and Allowed IPs, Save Peer and Apply Changes.",
  "UIShareHowMikroTik": "MikroTik: /interface/wireguard/peers/add interface=<your WireGuard interface> public-key=\"<public key>\" allowed-address=<tunnel IP>/32",
  "UIShareHowLinux": "Linux: append the [Peer] block to /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf, then run wg syncconf wg0 <(wg-quick strip wg0) as root.",
  "UIShareReply": "When it is done, please send me: my tunnel IP, the server's public address and port, and the server's public key. Do not send me any private key.",
  "UIShareTitle": "Send your public key to the administrator",
  "UIShareBody": "This is a ready message for the person who runs the server. It holds only public values; nothing in it is secret.",
  "UIExplainShareLong": "What happens here: the administrator must add your public key to the server before it lets this computer in. Send the message below by e-mail, copy it into a chat, or show the QR code. The administrator answers with your tunnel IP and the server's details, which you enter in the next step.",
